{
  "0": "Interest aggregation: for all four datasets, replace the current readout with a masked recency-weighted mean of the last five valid GRU states, using weights proportional to 2^(-age/2). Apply the same causal pooling at each supervised position. This may smooth noisy final states while retaining recent intent. Preserve history selection, embeddings, GRU topology, scoring, loss and optimizer; use one trained model per category.",
  "1": "Training strategy: for all four datasets, multiply the current learning rate by 0.7, preserving the optimizer, scheduler structure, batch size, dropout and weight decay. Smaller updates may reduce the early validation deterioration seen in Electronics while retaining progress in the other categories. Keep seed 42, the 200-epoch cap, existing stopping rules, model, readout, loss and evaluation unchanged.",
  "2": "Network architecture: for all four datasets, add one residual bottleneck inside the GRU backbone: transform each recurrent output h into h + W2(GELU(W1(h))), with bottleneck width max(1, floor(H/4)) and a zero-initialized W2. Feed these states to the unchanged readout. This initially preserves recurrent behavior while allowing a compact nonlinear refinement. Preserve embeddings, history, scoring, loss and optimizer; train one network per category.",
  "3": "Sequence modeling: for all four datasets, reduce the current maximum causal context length to max(1, ceil(0.75 * current_length)), retaining the most recent interactions in chronological order. Apply the identical cap during training and prediction. Removing the oldest quarter may reduce stale-interest interference and recurrent computation. Preserve supplied splits, features, embeddings, GRU topology, readout, scoring, loss and optimizer."
}
